From 4072059ccb92f2f7af28e7d68c5f626ed3b9f6ca Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nicolas De Carli <ndecarli@meta.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 18:02:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [VecLib] Add VecBool<T>: SVE comparisons return a predicate
 (#202420) (#202420)

Summary:

Introduce VecBool<T> class, which represents vectorized boolean values.

On SVE, VecBool<T> is represented using a predicate register type svbool_t. This allows better representation and usage of the SVE architecture.

For the other backends, VecBool<T> equals Vectorized<T>, with each lane being all 1s or all 0s. This is the current behaviour, thus only SVE builds are affected by the change.

Test Plan:
fbcode never builds the SVE256 capability, so I added a temporary `cpp_library` to `caffe2/TARGETS` (not included in this diff) that compiles `ATEN_NATIVE_CPU_KERNEL_CPP` plus the ufunc kernels with `-march=armv8.2-a+sve+bf16 -msve-vector-bits=256 -DCPU_CAPABILITY_SVE -DCPU_CAPABILITY_SVE256 -DCPU_CAPABILITY=SVE256 -DHAVE_SVE_CPU_DEFINITION`. It builds before and after this change:
```
buck2 build //caffe2:tmp_cpukernel_sve256 --keep-going
```
To find GCC-only breakage with clang, I also built that target with `VecBool` temporarily swapped for an operator-less struct. That flagged every predicate-op-predicate site, and those now go through `SveVecBool`'s operators.

GCC, matching the OSS CI `vec_test_all_types_SVE256` compile (its warning flags with `-Werror`, `-std=gnu++20 -O3 -march=armv8-a+sve+bf16 -D__ARM_FEATURE_BF16 -msve-vector-bits=256`), with gcc 15.2. It compiles with no warnings:
```
g++ -DCPU_CAPABILITY=SVE256 -DCPU_CAPABILITY_SVE256 -DHAVE_SVE_CPU_DEFINITION -DAT_BUILD_ARM_VEC256_WITH_SLEEF <CI warning flags> -Werror -std=gnu++20 -O3 -march=armv8-a+sve+bf16 -D__ARM_FEATURE_BF16 -msve-vector-bits=256 -fopenmp -c aten/src/ATen/test/vec_test_all_types.cpp
```

Standalone test of every comparison, `isnan`, `blendv`, `~`/`&`/`|`/`^`, mixed predicate/vector ops, `eq`/`lt`, `zero_mask` and `cast` for float, double, BFloat16 and int8..int64, against scalar references. The devserver's SVE vector length is 128 bits, so it runs on a copy of the headers with `VECTOR_WIDTH` set to 16; the real headers at 256 bits are compile-only:
```
clang++ / g++-15 -std=c++17 -O2 -march=armv8.2-a+sve+bf16 -msve-vector-bits=128 -DCPU_CAPABILITY_SVE -DCPU_CAPABILITY_SVE256 -DCPU_CAPABILITY=SVE256 t.cpp   # PASSED (0 failures) with both
clang++ / g++-15 ... -msve-vector-bits=256 -c t.cpp                                                                        # compiles with both
```
With clang the BFloat16 NaN lanes need `-fno-strict-aliasing`; the pre-change headers give the identical output, so that is an existing aliasing issue in SVE BFloat16 `loadu`/`store`, not this change.

NEON: `vec_test_all_types` passes 354 tests, and the same 3 BFloat16 `Fmadd`/`Fmsub` tests fail before and after this change (`Multiplication` and `Quantization8BitTests/1.Transpose` are excluded because they hit test-side UB/assertions):
```
buck2 build //caffe2:vec_test_all_types
vec_test_all_types --gtest_filter='-*Multiplication*:*Quantization8BitTests*.Transpose*'
```
x86 (generic `vec_base.h` path):
```
buck2 build -m ovr_config//cpu:x86_64 -m ovr_config//runtime/constraints:platform010 //caffe2:vec_test_all_types
```

